UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = REPLACE(Text, 'The ', '') 	WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_FEATURE_CRATER';
-----------------------------------------------------------
-- Original Civilopedia replacements
-----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'The Barringer Crater, also known as Meteor Crater, is a well-preserved impact site located near Winslow in northern Arizona, USA. Formed about 50,000 years ago during the Pleistocene epoch, it was created when a nickel–iron meteorite approximately 50 meters in diameter struck the Earth at a speed of several kilometers per second. The impact released energy equivalent to several megatons of TNT, excavating a bowl-shaped cavity about 1.2 kilometers wide and 170 meters deep. Although erosion has softened its features over time, the crater remains one of the best-preserved meteorite impact sites on Earth due to Arizona''s dry climate. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The site is named after Daniel Barringer, a mining engineer who, in the early 20th century, was the first to propose that the crater was the result of a meteorite impact rather than volcanic activity. Barringer spent decades studying the site, seeking to prove his theory and locate the meteorite''s remains. Today, the crater is privately owned by the Barringer family and managed as a scientific, educational, and tourist destination under the name Meteor Crater Natural Landmark. Visitors can explore an interactive museum, viewing platforms, and guided tours, while researchers continue to study the site as a natural laboratory for understanding planetary impacts and the role of meteorites in Earth''s history.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_BARRINGER_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'Cerro Potosi, often referred to as Cerro de Potosi or Cerro Rico ("Rich Mountain"), is a mountain in southern Bolivia that became one of the most famous sources of silver in world history. Discovered by Spanish colonizers in 1545, it quickly transformed the nearby settlement of Potosi into one of the wealthiest and most populous cities of the 16th and 17th centuries. Vast amounts of silver extracted from the mountain fueled the Spanish Empire''s global economy, playing a central role in Europe''s development and the expansion of international trade. However, this wealth came at a tremendous human cost, as the Spanish relied heavily on forced indigenous labor through the mita system, as well as enslaved Africans, leading to harsh conditions and widespread suffering in the mines. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Geographically, Cerro Potosi rises to about 4,824 meters above sea level and dominates the landscape of the Altiplano. The mountain itself is honeycombed with thousands of tunnels dug over centuries of continuous mining, earning it the reputation of being "eaten from within." Even today, mining continues, though on a smaller scale and under dangerous conditions, as the rich veins of silver have largely been depleted. Cerro Potosi stands as both a symbol of extraordinary natural wealth and a reminder of colonial exploitation. Recognized as part of the UNESCO World Heritage Site of Potosi, the mountain holds deep historical, cultural, and economic significance, representing both the grandeur and the tragedy of resource extraction in Latin American history.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_POTOSI_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'El Dorado originated as a legend among early Spanish explorers in South America during the 16th century. The term, meaning "The Gilded One", first referred to a Muisca ritual in present-day Colombia, in which a new ruler would cover his body in gold dust and immerse himself in Lake Guatavita as an offering to the gods. Reports of this ceremony, combined with tales of immense wealth, soon evolved into the myth of a golden city or kingdom hidden deep within the uncharted interior of the continent. Driven by greed and ambition, Spanish conquistadors and later European adventurers launched numerous expeditions into the Andes, Amazon, and Orinoco regions, often enduring great hardship and loss in their fruitless quests to find El Dorado. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Over time, El Dorado became less about a single place and more about the enduring idea of a land of unimaginable riches. Although no such golden city was ever found, the legend had a profound historical impact, fueling European exploration, conquest, and colonization of South America. Today, El Dorado is remembered as both a cautionary tale of obsession and exploitation and as a powerful cultural symbol representing humanity''s longing for wealth, discovery, and the unattainable. Its story continues to inspire literature, art, and popular imagination, illustrating how myth can shape history as strongly as tangible events.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_EL_DORADO_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'The Fountain of Youth is a legendary spring said to restore youth and vitality to anyone who drinks or bathes in its waters. The myth traces back to antiquity, with versions appearing in classical and medieval cultures, but it became most famous during the Age of Exploration in the 16th century. Spanish accounts linked the legend to the explorer Juan Ponce de Leon, who, according to later stories, sought the miraculous spring while searching for new lands in the Caribbean and Florida. While there is little evidence that Ponce de Leon actually pursued such a fountain—his expeditions were more likely motivated by territorial expansion and colonization—the association endured, embedding the tale deeply in European and American folklore. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]As a cultural symbol, the Fountain of Youth represents humanity''s timeless fascination with immortality, rejuvenation, and the defiance of aging. The legend has been localized to different parts of the world, but Florida remains most closely tied to the story, with tourist attractions—such as the Fountain of Youth Archaeological Park in St. Augustine—keeping the myth alive. Though never proven real, the fountain continues to capture the imagination, inspiring art, literature, and popular media. It endures less as a geographical mystery than as a metaphor for the universal human desire to recover lost youth and to transcend the natural limits of life.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_FOUNTAIN_YOUTH_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'The Grand Mesa, located in western Colorado, USA, is recognized as the largest flat-topped mountain in the world. Rising more than 10,000 feet (about 3,000 meters) above sea level and covering an area of roughly 500 square miles, it was formed millions of years ago through volcanic activity. Lava flows hardened into a basalt cap that protected the softer sedimentary layers beneath from erosion, leaving behind the vast plateau seen today. Indigenous peoples, including the Ute, lived in and around the area for centuries, relying on its forests, game, and lakes for sustenance. In the late 19th and early 20th centuries, settlers moved in, drawn by opportunities for ranching, logging, and recreation, further shaping the human history of the region. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Today, the Grand Mesa is part of the Grand Mesa National Forest, established in 1892, and is renowned for its natural beauty and recreational opportunities. Its expansive surface hosts over 300 lakes, dense spruce-fir forests, and diverse wildlife, making it a popular destination for fishing, hiking, camping, and skiing at the Powderhorn Mountain Resort. The mesa''s flat summit provides sweeping panoramic views of the Rocky Mountains, the Colorado River Valley, and the surrounding canyonlands. Beyond its scenic and ecological importance, the Grand Mesa stands as a striking geological feature that highlights the region''s volcanic past and continues to serve as a cultural and recreational landmark in Colorado.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_GRANDMESA_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'The Great Barrier Reef, stretching more than 2,300 kilometers along the northeastern coast of Australia, is the largest coral reef system in the world and one of Earth''s most remarkable natural wonders. It began forming around 20,000 years ago after the last Ice Age, when rising sea levels flooded the continental shelf, allowing corals to grow and accumulate over millennia. Indigenous Australian groups, including the Torres Strait Islanders and Aboriginal peoples, have lived along the reef for tens of thousands of years, developing deep cultural, spiritual, and practical connections with its marine resources. The reef was first charted by Europeans in the 18th century, most famously by Captain James Cook in 1770, and since then it has become a central symbol of Australia''s natural heritage. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ecologically, the Great Barrier Reef is a vast and complex system made up of over 2,900 individual reefs and 900 islands, supporting an extraordinary diversity of life. It is home to thousands of species, including corals, fish, sea turtles, seabirds, and marine mammals, making it one of the richest ecosystems on the planet. Designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1981, the reef is also a major global tourist attraction, contributing significantly to Australia''s economy. However, it faces severe threats from climate change, coral bleaching, pollution, and overfishing, which endanger its long-term survival. Today, the Great Barrier Reef stands as both a symbol of natural beauty and biodiversity and as a critical reminder of the urgent need for global environmental stewardship.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_GREATBARRIERREEF_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'Mount Kilimanjaro, located in northeastern Tanzania near the border with Kenya, is the tallest mountain in Africa, rising to 5,895 meters (19,341 feet) above sea level. It is a dormant stratovolcano composed of three distinct volcanic cones: Kibo, Mawenzi, and Shira. Geological studies suggest that the mountain began forming around 3 million years ago during the Great Rift Valley''s tectonic activity. For centuries, Kilimanjaro was known to local Chagga people, who wove it into their cultural traditions and stories. Europeans first documented the mountain in the 19th century, with German missionary Johannes Rebmann reporting its snow-capped peak in 1848—an account initially met with skepticism in Europe. Later, it became a focal point of colonial exploration and was eventually incorporated into German East Africa, then British rule, before becoming part of independent Tanzania in 1961. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Today, Mount Kilimanjaro is one of the most iconic landmarks on the continent, renowned for its striking silhouette and ecological diversity. Its slopes encompass multiple climate zones, from tropical forests at its base to alpine deserts and glaciers at its summit. The mountain attracts tens of thousands of climbers each year, as reaching the summit—Uhuru Peak—requires no technical climbing skills, making it one of the most accessible of the world''s highest peaks. Kilimanjaro also holds ecological and scientific importance, though its glaciers have been retreating rapidly due to climate change, raising concerns about the loss of this unique natural feature. Beyond its physical grandeur, Kilimanjaro remains a cultural symbol of endurance, freedom, and natural beauty, immortalized in literature, art, and national identity.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_KILIMANJARO_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'King Solomon''s Mines is a legendary concept that first gained wide fame through literature, rather than archaeology or verified history. The idea was popularized by British author H. Rider Haggard in his 1885 adventure novel King Solomon''s Mines, which tells the tale of a lost, treasure-filled mine supposedly belonging to the biblical King Solomon. The story combined biblical references, African exploration, and the allure of hidden riches, captivating Victorian readers at a time when European colonial powers were deeply invested in mapping and exploiting Africa. While inspired by biblical mentions of Solomon''s wealth, there is no historical evidence that such mines ever existed; instead, the tale grew out of a mix of myth, speculation, and the colonial imagination. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]As a description, "King Solomon''s Mines" has since become shorthand for any mysterious or fabulously wealthy lost treasure site. The legend is often tied to southern Africa, particularly because of the region''s rich gold and diamond deposits, which fueled speculation that they could be the biblical source of Solomon''s wealth. Over time, explorers, treasure hunters, and writers alike have searched for or reimagined the mines in both fact and fiction. Today, the mines remain firmly in the realm of legend and popular culture, inspiring countless novels, films, and stories that blend adventure, mystery, and the enduring human fascination with hidden riches.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_SOLOMONS_MINES_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'Krakatoa (also spelled Krakatau) is a volcanic island located in the Sunda Strait between Java and Sumatra in Indonesia. It became world-famous for its catastrophic eruption in August 1883, one of the most violent volcanic events in recorded history. The explosion destroyed most of the island, unleashed massive tsunamis, and generated shockwaves that circled the globe multiple times. The eruption''s sound was reportedly heard as far as Australia and the Indian Ocean island of Rodrigues, over 4,800 kilometers away. An estimated 36,000 people lost their lives, mostly due to the tsunamis, and the atmospheric effects of the eruption—including vivid sunsets and a measurable drop in global temperatures—were observed worldwide for several years afterward. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Today, Krakatoa is remembered both as a geological marvel and a reminder of nature''s destructive power. The volcano did not vanish completely; instead, a new cone called Anak Krakatau ("Child of Krakatoa") emerged from the caldera in 1927 and has been growing and erupting periodically ever since. The surrounding area remains volcanically active, with eruptions continuing into the 21st century. Beyond its scientific importance as a site for studying volcanology and climate impacts, Krakatoa has also entered cultural memory, inspiring books, films, and art that highlight both its destructive force and its role in shaping global awareness of natural disasters.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_KRAKATOA_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'Lake Victoria, located in East Africa, is the largest lake on the continent and the world''s largest tropical lake, covering an area of about 68,800 square kilometers. It lies within the borders of Tanzania, Uganda, and Kenya, and serves as the principal reservoir of the Nile River. The lake was formed thousands of years ago in a shallow depression within the East African Rift system. For centuries, it has been central to the livelihoods, trade, and cultural traditions of the diverse communities living along its shores. In the mid-19th century, it gained international attention when European explorers, most notably John Hanning Speke in 1858, identified it as the source of the White Nile, sparking great interest in the geography and colonial ambitions of Africa. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Today, Lake Victoria plays a vital role in the region''s economy and ecology, supporting one of the world''s largest inland fisheries and providing water, transport, and hydroelectric power for millions of people. The lake is home to an extraordinary variety of species, especially cichlid fish, though this biodiversity has been severely threatened by human activity, pollution, and the introduction of invasive species such as the Nile perch. The lake has also faced challenges like declining water levels and ecological degradation due to climate change and overuse. Despite these pressures, Lake Victoria remains a cornerstone of East African life, symbolizing both the region''s natural wealth and the urgent need for sustainable management of shared resources.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_LAKE_VICTORIA_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'Mount Fuji, located on Honshu Island, is Japan''s tallest mountain at 3,776 meters (12,389 feet) and one of its most iconic landmarks. It is a stratovolcano that last erupted in 1707 during the Edo period, an event known as the H?ei eruption, which scattered ash as far as modern-day Tokyo. Geologically, Mount Fuji formed over hundreds of thousands of years through successive volcanic activity, with its current symmetrical cone developing around 10,000 years ago. The mountain has held deep cultural and spiritual significance for centuries, revered in Shinto and Buddhist traditions as a sacred site. It became a subject of pilgrimage, poetry, and art, most famously depicted in Katsushika Hokusai''s Thirty-Six Views of Mount Fuji during the early 19th century. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]In the modern era, Mount Fuji continues to be a powerful national symbol of Japan, representing beauty, strength, and resilience. It was designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2013 for both its cultural and spiritual importance. Each year, hundreds of thousands of climbers ascend the mountain during the official climbing season in July and August, while millions more view it from afar. The mountain''s landscape includes five surrounding lakes, shrines, and natural hot springs, making it a major tourist attraction. Despite its dormant status, scientists monitor Mount Fuji closely, as its history suggests potential for future eruptions. Today, it stands not only as a geological wonder but also as a living emblem of Japan''s identity, blending natural majesty with centuries of human reverence.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_FUJI_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'Mount Kailash, rising to 6,638 meters (21,778 feet) in the Trans-Himalayan range of western Tibet, is one of the world''s most revered sacred mountains. Unlike other famous peaks, it has never been climbed, as it is considered holy in multiple religious traditions. In Hinduism, it is believed to be the abode of Lord Shiva, where he resides in eternal meditation with his consort Parvati. In Buddhism, it is associated with Demchok, a representation of supreme bliss, while in Jainism it marks the site where the first Tirthankara attained liberation. For followers of the ancient Bon religion of Tibet, it is considered the seat of spiritual power. Pilgrims from across Asia have journeyed to the mountain for centuries, performing the ritual kora—a 52-kilometer circumambulation around its base—as an act of devotion and purification. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Geographically, Mount Kailash stands isolated yet striking, with its distinct pyramid-like shape dominating the landscape near the sources of some of Asia''s great rivers, including the Indus, Brahmaputra, Sutlej, and Karnali (a tributary of the Ganges). The surrounding region is stark yet spiritually significant, with the nearby Lake Manasarovar and Lake Rakshastal also holding deep mythological meaning. Because of its sacred status, climbing the mountain is prohibited, preserving it as a pristine site of pilgrimage and reverence rather than conquest. Today, Mount Kailash remains not only a natural wonder but also a unique convergence point of spirituality, geography, and culture, symbolizing humanity''s quest for transcendence and harmony with the divine.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_MT_KAILASH_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'Mount Sinai, located on the Sinai Peninsula of Egypt and rising to about 2,285 meters (7,497 feet), is one of the most important sacred mountains in the world. It is traditionally identified in Judaism, Christianity, and Islam as the mountain where Moses received the Ten Commandments from God, a defining event in the Abrahamic faiths. The mountain has been a site of pilgrimage for centuries, with early Christian hermits and monks establishing a spiritual presence there as early as the 3rd century CE. In the 6th century, the Byzantine Emperor Justinian I ordered the construction of St. Catherine''s Monastery at its base, one of the oldest working Christian monasteries in the world, which continues to be a center of worship, scholarship, and preservation of sacred manuscripts. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Physically, Mount Sinai is a rugged, rocky peak set within a stark desert landscape of granite mountains. Pilgrims and visitors often climb its trails to reach the summit, where a small chapel and a mosque stand as symbols of its shared religious significance. The mountain offers sweeping views of the surrounding arid terrain, enhancing its aura of solitude and spirituality. Today, it remains a place of devotion and reflection for believers and a destination for travelers drawn by its biblical heritage and dramatic natural setting. Mount Sinai''s enduring importance lies in its dual identity as both a sacred site of divine revelation and a striking landmark in the geography of the Middle East.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_MT_SINAI_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'Old Faithful, located in Yellowstone National Park in the United States, is one of the most famous geysers in the world. It was named in 1870 by members of the Washburn–Langford–Doane Expedition, who marveled at its frequent and seemingly reliable eruptions. Old Faithful became the first geyser in the park to receive an official name, and since then it has stood as a symbol of Yellowstone and America''s natural wonders. Yellowstone itself was designated the world''s first national park in 1872, and Old Faithful quickly became one of its most iconic attractions, drawing visitors from around the globe to witness its spectacular bursts of steam and water. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Geologically, Old Faithful is a cone geyser powered by the immense geothermal activity beneath Yellowstone, which sits atop a volcanic hotspot. Its eruptions typically last 1.5 to 5 minutes, shooting between 3,700 and 8,400 gallons of boiling water up to 185 feet into the air. While not perfectly predictable, eruptions occur on average every 60 to 110 minutes, making it one of the most dependable geysers in the park. Surrounding Old Faithful is the historic Old Faithful Inn, built in 1904, which has provided generations of visitors with a vantage point to enjoy the geyser''s performance. Today, Old Faithful continues to embody the dynamic geological forces of Yellowstone while serving as a natural spectacle that bridges science, history, and public wonder.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_OLDFAITHFUL_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'The Rock of Gibraltar, a towering limestone promontory at the southern tip of the Iberian Peninsula, has been a landmark of strategic and cultural significance for millennia. Rising about 426 meters (1,398 feet) above sea level, it guards the narrow Strait of Gibraltar, the crucial passage between the Mediterranean Sea and the Atlantic Ocean. Known to the ancient Greeks and Romans as one of the Pillars of Hercules, it marked the edge of the known world in classical mythology. In later centuries, its location made it a prize in regional power struggles. The Rock came under Moorish control in the 8th century, was later taken by the Spanish, and finally seized by the British in 1704 during the War of the Spanish Succession. It was formally ceded to Britain by the Treaty of Utrecht in 1713, beginning its long history as a key British naval base. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Today, the Rock of Gibraltar is not only a symbol of resilience and strategic power but also a natural and cultural landmark. It houses a labyrinth of military tunnels carved during conflicts such as the Great Siege (1779–1783) and World War II, reflecting its enduring defensive value. The Rock is also famous for its colony of Barbary macaques, the only wild monkeys in Europe, which have become a popular attraction. Its dramatic cliffs, caves like St. Michael''s, and panoramic views make it a destination for both tourists and historians. At the crossroads of Europe and Africa, the Rock of Gibraltar continues to embody both geopolitical importance and natural beauty, bridging continents and histories alike.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_GIBRALTER_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'Sri Pada, also known as Adam''s Peak, is a sacred mountain in central Sri Lanka that rises to 2,243 meters (7,359 feet) above sea level. It has been a revered pilgrimage site for over a thousand years due to the large footprint-shaped impression found near its summit, which different faiths attribute to divine figures. In Buddhist tradition, it is believed to be the footprint of the Buddha; Hindus regard it as belonging to Lord Shiva; Muslims and Christians often associate it with Adam, who, according to legend, first set foot on Earth there after being cast out of paradise. Pilgrimages to Sri Pada are recorded as early as the 4th century CE, when the Chinese monk Faxian visited the site, and it has remained a symbol of interfaith reverence ever since. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The mountain is also renowned for its natural beauty and the striking phenomenon known as the "sacred shadow" or "triangular shadow"—a perfect triangular shadow the peak casts at sunrise, even though the mountain is not perfectly conical. Each year, from December to May, thousands of pilgrims and tourists climb its 5,000 or more steps, often at night, to witness the sunrise from the summit. The surrounding area, rich in biodiversity, is part of the Peak Wilderness Sanctuary, a UNESCO World Heritage Site within the Central Highlands of Sri Lanka. Today, Sri Pada stands as both a spiritual beacon and a natural wonder, embodying the harmony of faith, culture, and landscape.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_SRI_PADA_TEXT';
UPDATE Language_en_US SET Text = 'Uluru, also known as Ayers Rock, is a massive sandstone monolith located in the heart of Australia''s Northern Territory, within Uluru-Kata Tjuta National Park. Rising 348 meters (1,142 feet) above the surrounding desert plain and stretching about 9 kilometers in circumference, it is one of the most iconic natural landmarks of Australia. Uluru was formed around 550 million years ago during geological upheavals that shaped central Australia. For tens of thousands of years, it has been a sacred site for the Anangu, the region''s Aboriginal people, who are among the world''s oldest continuous cultures. The rock features prominently in their Tjukurpa (Dreamtime) stories, which explain its origins, spiritual significance, and connection to ancestral beings. European explorer William Gosse first documented Uluru in 1873, naming it Ayers Rock after Sir Henry Ayers, the Chief Secretary of South Australia, though its traditional name has since been officially restored. [NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Today, Uluru is celebrated both as a geological wonder and as a place of profound cultural importance. Its surface changes color dramatically at different times of the day, glowing red at sunrise and sunset, making it a magnet for visitors from around the world. The site is jointly managed by the Anangu people and Parks Australia, ensuring the preservation of both its natural environment and cultural traditions. In 1987, Uluru was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site for its natural values, and in 1994 it received a second listing for its cultural significance. Climbing the rock was officially banned in 2019 out of respect for Anangu traditions, marking a major step in recognizing Indigenous rights. Uluru remains a powerful symbol of Australia''s natural heritage and Indigenous spirituality, embodying the deep connection between people and land.' WHERE Tag = 'TXT_KEY_CIV5_FEATURES_ULURU_TEXT';
